<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: The Red Pens
</h4>

<p>
Quick thoughts:<br />
<br />
Make a promise to the reader at the beginning, something they will find out. The ending’s given away too soon at the top, almost like a nut graf, and then it goes on to describe life at the motel. <br />
<br />
I’d want to see what things looked like from the customer’s viewpoint, then bring in the anecdote, then that magic quote: “The guys want to believe it so bad,” Nelson says. “They want to believe there is whores in here. But it ain’t fucking so.” <br />
So the reader’s drawn in. Everyone seems to think that this place caters to that clientele, why not build up that expectation, then dash it with the quote. <br />
<br />
Overall, good structure.<br />
<br />
On diction, I can’t see the stump “fluttering,” seems like there should be a better word. Is it like a wing he’s flapping? Or maybe like a weapon he’s brandishing? <br />
<br />
Good use of quotes, sprinkled throughout for effect and scene. But this guy, there’s no way he doesn’t have an accent. If you’re writing for people who would be surprised that he drops his r’s or lets his a’s carry, I’d want to hear more of that. Also from the bartenders, especially the foreign ones, what they sound like. <br />
<br />
TONS of good reporting here, but at times the narrative strays from the theme, namely, that all kinds of people think there’s prostitutes at this place, and there’s no dissuading them. Tightening it up, maybe lose a section, and it would improve the flow. <br />
<br />
Nice work. 
</p>
